1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,188
Anyone confined to bed, after a stroke, surgery or a long illness, can develop

2
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:09,774
pressure wounds within days. Most of them are older adults, and ageing societies

3
00:00:09,824 --> 00:00:13,301
like Japan, Thailand and England will have more every year.

4
00:00:13,351 --> 00:00:17,886
They're cared for at home by family without clinical training, and nurses can't

5
00:00:17,936 --> 00:00:19,650
see every wound every day.

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,088
WoundMeter is my entry for Build for Your World:

7
00:00:22,138 --> 00:00:24,743
it brings Meta's models into elderly care, the field my research comes from.

8
00:00:24,793 --> 00:00:27,193
It's not an AI doctor and it gives no advice to patients.

9
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:29,848
It turns a phone photo into a calibrated measurement and documentation that a

10
00:00:29,898 --> 00:00:32,298
nurse or physician verifies. Everything in this video was built during the

11
00:00:32,348 --> 00:00:33,524
hackathon, except two parts I'll flag:

12
00:00:33,574 --> 00:00:35,566
my earlier YOLO wound model and the basic ruler measurement.

13
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:38,629
I adapted some code from that prototype, but most of it is new, written this

14
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:39,650
week with AI coding assistance.

15
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:43,241
Here's a sample photo with the printed ruler.

16
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:47,728
Straightening the photo onto the ruler's scale comes from my earlier prototype.

17
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:52,963
What's new is Meta SAM 3.1. It outlines the whole wound bed, including slough.

18
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:58,946
I built the SAM integration, a decoder for its mask format, and a step where my

19
00:00:58,996 --> 00:01:04,928
older YOLO model only points SAM to the right place, so it ignores pen marks and

20
00:01:04,978 --> 00:01:09,415
scars. The numbers are computed by code, never by a language model.

21
00:01:09,465 --> 00:01:14,650
If the outline is wrong, the clinician drags it, and the area re-measures live.

22
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:23,164
Muse Spark 1.3 drafts the documentation from the measured figures and describes

23
00:01:23,214 --> 00:01:27,780
what's visible; it never diagnoses or stages.

24
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:36,353
The clinician accepts, edits or rejects the draft, and signs with four explicit

25
00:01:36,403 --> 00:01:39,650
confirmations. AI never approves anything.

26
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:45,473
Eight weeks later the wound has closed.

27
00:01:45,523 --> 00:01:51,442
Instead of failing, WoundMeter reports no visible wound.

28
00:01:51,492 --> 00:01:59,650
Zero square centimetres is recorded only after a clinician confirms it.

29
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:03,850
Approved visits build the wound's history:

30
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:11,050
here the verified area falls from eighteen square centimetres to under one.

31
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:18,850
The record assistant uses Muse Spark with tool calling to answer questions from

32
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,250
approved data, and nurses can dictate with Muse Voice.

33
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:29,650
The family sees only what a clinician has approved.

34
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:34,631
To recap: before the hackathon I had the YOLO model and the basic ruler.

35
00:02:34,681 --> 00:02:38,698
Everything else, the Meta SAM 3.1, Muse Spark and Muse Voice integrations, the

36
00:02:38,748 --> 00:02:41,827
boundary editor, clinician sign-off, the no-visible-wound workflow and the care

37
00:02:41,877 --> 00:02:46,521
platform, I built in these ten days, mostly as new code written with AI coding

38
00:02:46,571 --> 00:02:49,650
assistance. The repository is public and lists it in detail.

39
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,637
WoundMeter: AI assists, families care, professionals decide.

40
00:02:54,687 --> 00:02:59,650
Try it at woundmeter dot wtepsan dot dev.

